Reviewing my earlier answer, I found it was seriously flawed. The ship that laid the 1866 transatlantic cable was the **SS Great Eastern**, Isambard Kingdom Brunel's massive iron steamship, chartered by entrepreneur Cyrus Field. The Great Eastern was used because it was the only vessel large enough to carry the entire cable (~2,000+ miles) in one run. It departed from Valentia, Ireland, and landed the cable at Heart's Content, Newfoundland, in July 1866. It also recovered and completed the 1865 cable.

My earlier answer named the "Great Western," which was a different, earlier Brunel ship and had nothing to do with the 1866 cable. The supporting details I gave (departure from Halifax, Captain William L. Scott, Western Union) were also inaccurate—the venture was run by the Atlantic Telegraph Company.

