Reviewing my earlier response, I need to correct it. The word game that became Scrabble was invented by **Alfred Mosher Butts**, an architect from New York. In the early 1930s he developed the game under the names **Lexiko** (also known as "Criss-Cross Words"). He studied letter frequencies in English texts (using the front page of the *New York Times*) to determine letter distributions and point values.

James Brunot's actual role: Butts licensed the game to entrepreneur James Brunot (who had played it), and Brunot refined the rules, added the premium squares, chose the name "Scrabble," and began manufacturing it in 1948. Brunot was the producer/marketer, **not the original inventor**. So my earlier answer confused the two men.

